.TH PIP 5
.SH NAME
pip \- the Osprey session protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #O/pip
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Pip is the protocol Osprey containers are spoken to with.  What it keeps
from 9P: authentication is someone else's business, fids name what a
conversation is working on, and every T message is answered by an R
message carrying its tag.  What it changes is three things.
.PP
A
.I session
outlives the connection that carried it.  It is named by a number, and a
client whose connection has gone opens another and says which session it
is resuming; its fids are still there.
.PP
Transactions are
.IR grouped .
As many T messages as will fit are written at once and their R messages
read back at once, so that opening a thing, reading it and closing it is
one round trip rather than three.
.PP
What is read from a thing is
.IR named .
A read asks for an attribute by string rather than for bytes at an
offset, and the attribute called
.B *
is the list of attributes the thing has.
.SH MESSAGES
.PP
Numbers are little endian.  A string is a two-byte length followed by
that many bytes, not terminated.  Every message begins
.IP
.nf
size[4] type[1] tag[2]
.fi
.PP
and the rest is
.IP
.nf
Tsession	sid[8]		resume that session, or 0 for a new one
Rsession	sid[8]
Topen		fid[4] nfid[4] how[1] name[s]
Ropen		qid[8]
Tread		fid[4] count[4] attr[s]
Rread		version[8] data[s]
Twrite		fid[4] attr[s] data[s]
Rwrite		version[8]
Tclose		fid[4]
Rclose
Rerror		ename[s]
.fi
.PP
.B Topen
walks the name from the root, clones
.I fid
into
.I nfid
and opens it: the walk, the clone and the open of 9P in one message.  A
group begins with
.BR Tsession ,
and the session it settles on is remembered against the connection, so a
client need not name it again in every group.
.PP
A
.I version
counts the writes to what a fid names, so that a client can tell whether
what it read is still what is there.
.SH NAMES
.PP
The root is
.BR / ,
and its only attribute is
.BR * ,
the list of the containers there are.  A container is named by its
number.  Its attributes are those
.IR osprey (3)
reports:
.BR core ,
.BR state ,
.BR budget ,
.BR used ,
.BR period ,
.BR deadline ,
.BR besteffort ,
.BR periods ,
.BR mode ,
.BR memory ,
.BR memused ,
.BR netbytes ,
.BR netmax ,
.BR packets ,
.BR requests ,
.BR replies ,
.BR slice ,
.BR data ,
.B pid
and
.BR version .
Of those,
.BR budget ,
.BR period ,
.BR deadline ,
.BR memory ,
.B netmax
and
.B mode
may be written; the rest are what the container has done rather than what
it was asked to do.
.SH EXAMPLE
.PP
Open a container, read every attribute it has and close it, in one round
trip:
.IP
.nf
pip open 2 /5 read 2 '*' read 2 budget close 2
.fi
.PP
Come back to the same session from another process, with the fid still
open:
.IP
.nf
pip -s 1 write 2 mode strict read 2 version
.fi
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/osprey/pip.h
.br
.B /os/osprey/devosprey.c
.br
.B /os/osprey/cmd/pip.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR osprey (3)
.SH BUGS
.PP
The presentation also has files leased, and versions immutable and
committed when a file is closed.  Here a version is only a count of the
writes, nothing is immutable, and nothing is leased.
.PP
Pip is spoken over a file rather than being its own transport, and there
is no isochronous data.  The things it names are containers; no file
system is served over it.
.PP
A session is remembered against the connection that last used it, so two
connections working one session at once will confuse each other's
replies.  Eight sessions of sixteen fids are all there are, and a session
is never collected: it lasts until the kernel is rebooted.
